udents labor under the assumption  that if there are objective standards of  right and wrong  they should be immediately clear to  everybody  and if they're not immediately clear to  everybody  then there are no objective standards of  right and wrong so what would this be  this would be a false dichotomy wouldn't  it it's saying  either there are objective standards of  right and wrong  and they are immediately intuitive or  evident to everybody and there therefore  everybody would follow them  which you know is not the case or  everybody's just got their own  individual perspective and there really  is no objective right and wrong there's  just subjective preferences or  opinions or experiences or life you know  pick whatever you like you know whatever  jargon you like over there  is that all there is could it could  there be other possibilities  could there be the possibility that  maybe there are  objective standards of right and wrong  but that it takes a lot of work  to be able to actually figure out what  they are  and to apply them correctly and to to  make you know  good judgments and difficult cases if  there's a third possibility  then you've got a false dichotomy and if  you've got a false dichotomy  and you stick to that false dichotomy  you are going to find yourself  stuck and unable to make any any  progress  you're probably going to make things  difficult for other people too if you're  called upon to make any  decisions that way a third one that  comes up which  really exemplifies this this third type  of blurring things together i realize  the case that we just looked at  also exemplifies that blurring things  together too  some people will say um  moral values opinions about right and  wrong  pick whatever you like in that sort of  category  experiences the bases on which we we  choose to make decisions  these are all individual sometimes  they'll say things like they're unique  to every individual which which is  clearly nonsense right because  if they were in fact unique to every  individual then  there couldn't be any discussion of them  whatsoever experiences  may to some degree be said to be unique  but moral values no there's a certain  array of them  and you may try to come up with  something new but whatever you pick  guaranteed plenty of other people have  picked that particular thing to orient  their moral decision making around  and so you're not unique that's just  nonsense that people tell themselves  because we live in a culture that wants  to preach that to us  be that as it may it is true that people  do have individual perspectives  that uh the same person like aristotle  points out  two thousand years ago does not always  necessarily  think the same things about the same  things particularly if they don't have  good moral character they're going to be  all over the map  so does that solve the issue of whether  morality  itself or you know moral values  themselves are all  purely subjective that's a different  question isn't it  it's one thing to say or to ask the  question  do people differ on moral issues  do they arrive at different moral  judgments do they begin from different  sets of values  have they had different experiences  that's one  set of questions it's an entirely  different animal  to say are  moral values purely subjective  or even if we want to go a little bit  further are they just relative to  societies or groups or  that sort of thing and if you blur these  questions together  if you're not careful in distinguishing  them  apart from each other and asking for  answers  separately from them then when you  answer one  you're going to think that you've  answered the other one you're going to  think that you've answered both  and that may not be the case so  you know why do i bring this up in these  particular examples because a lot of  students come to my classes  and frankly you know quite a few people  that i enter into conversation with  come to me with the views that  everything is just purely you know  subjective because  they've been misled by faulty reasoning  by not making distinctions distinctions  are one of the fundamental tools  of human thought if you're not willing  to make distinctions  you're not willing to think very far  you're only willing to think in  in very simplistic abstract terms if you  want to understand difficult matters the  sort of things that people do disagree  about  you have to be prepared to make  distinctions and to them to follow  through the different paths and see  where they lead to  now let's look at some of the reasons  why  people don't make distinctions when they  when they need to be making distinctions  in order to think things through  why does this occur one reason  is because of a lack of information  people simply don't  know that there are differentiations  that they should be looking at that not  everything is exactly the same  and to a certain extent people can be  held  you know less responsible  for that provided that the information  isn't missing because they decided to  disregard it  or because they are sloppy in the way  that they  they handle information they were given  it but didn't pay attention to it  you know here would be an example most  americans know very little about  islam as a a religion  as a sort of culture and civilization  spanning centuries i think  most americans by this point hopefully  do know that there  there's two main divisions within islam  sunni islam which is the largest and  then shia islam there's others as well  you know other distinctions that get  made and within each of them  you can find other further distinctions  that are being made  now let's say that you actually do know  okay there's these two different kinds  of  of muslims and they don't like each  other necessarily  but that's it is that all that you need  to know  about islam in order to  think things through when it comes to  actually dealing with muslims  well um why are things different from  from muslim country to muslim country  why aren't they all exactly the same  because there are distinctions that are  made for example  there are four main legal schools within  sunni islam  and they have different ways of you know  looking at  at the particular issues of reasoning  things out  so if you didn't have that information  you wouldn't know that  now once you actually do acquire that  information  then you can start making better and  better distinctions you can start  thinking things through more carefully  you can start asking the right sorts of  questions  and that leads to the second  oftentimes people have the habit  of stopping short in their research and  this could be because of a lack of  curiosity  or a you know unwillingness to think  things through  or wanting to get things done very very  quickly or being kind of sloppy  it could actually come from a lack of  information about what is research  and how much information is there out  there and what you know  what ought you to be doing as a human  being carrying that sort of stuff  out but very often people will not go  further than they they feel that they  should or further than they feel that  that they need to  uh in order to think about how things  split up how things break down and so  they'll engage in  you know sort of sloppy simplistic  abstract thinking or they'll set up  false dichotomies because they they  don't realize  by going further that there are other  alternatives or they'll  blur things together because they didn't  really think that these are separate  issues  when a little bit more research would  indicate that to them  now a lot of times people don't know  where to go  this is another problem but  again these two kind of go together  they're not researching the lack of  information  they're also consolidated by mental  habits and this is a very important  point  as human beings we are the product of  our actions and choices  and those get consolidated into habits  this is not entirely up to us we are  formed to some degree by  our environment and the the things that  other people provide us as models  um we don't you know it's not as if you  can snap your fingers overnight and quit  whatever habit is that you have  but whether it comes to physical actions  like biting one's fingernails or whether  it comes to emotional responses  or ways of thinking or ways of  approaching things  these are matters of habit and the more  that you do something a particular way  the more it's going to seem natural to  you and the more  other ways of doing it are going to seem  unnatural or they're going to be totally  left out of the picture  so if you get yourself in the habit of  doing these sorts of things  and i think unfortunately that the way  that we educate a lot of the time  tends to produce and reward  some of this cognitive behavior  on a lot of people's parts in part  because i think educators themselves  often don't make good distinctions when  they want to be making them  because we do this a lot of people have  mental habits  that make it difficult for them to not  only make distinctions but to see the  need to make distinctions and  what is the result of that they don't  make the distinctions  and then they wind up in all sorts of  quandaries operas you know literally  where you can't find your way out  when actually if you made the right  distinctions and then followed them  through  you could think your way through  something  i think that emotions also fit in with  this as well  people often have a very strong desire  for things to be simpler than they are  and  i can understand that you know i feel i  fall into that myself  from time to time when i when i over  simplify  in in my personal life but it's  something that we have to resist  if we want to understand reality reality  is complicated  even a single human being is very  complicated  so when it comes to ethics why would we  possibly think that everything could be  reduced to just a simple  list of cut and dried answers  people want that sort of thing but what  they're desiring is something that's  actually not  good for them that's a starting point  being given a code  but that's not really becoming a thinker  who can actually apply  ethical or moral principles to real  cases and successfully  come up with the good moral evaluations  and judgments and then  be able to justify them to other people  to get them on the same  on the same track or to figure out some  of these you know big issues that that  keep coming up over and over and over  again  which we have to address so  all of these are things that if you want  to think about where you  actually stand with this would be good  to  focus on do you feel like things  ought to be simpler than they are do you  feel a kind of repugnance when being  asked to make distinctions  is that coming about because of emotions  or is that coming about because of  mental habits  have you actually sought out information  when you wind up in some sort of  quandary  do you know that it's up to you to go  out and look for answers  that the answers are not just going to  pop into your head  you may be lucky enough to have some  people near you who actually see the  problem that you're in and say  oh i've had this sort of problem myself  but most of the time as an adult you  actually have to seek out the answers  that you need  and making distinctions is going to be  an absolutely irreplaceable tool  in your arsenal in order to be able to  do that  so  if i had to close on anything i would  just say  you can't get by without making good  distinctions  the more that the more that you practice  that the more that you get used to doing  that  the better you're going to be from  situation to situation to situation from  subject matter to subject matter to  subject matter  because this is an extremely important  cognitive skill  not only for ethics but for anything  that you're going to go into  and i'm willing to bet whatever it is  that you're interested in whatever it is  that you're studying if it's not ethics  you've probably learned how to make  distinctions in that  area if you're really interested in  cooking you don't think all spices are  the same right  so you actually make distinctions and  you say well this is for this case this  is for this case  learn how to transfer that skill from  one context to the other  and you're putting yourself in really  good stead to be able to handle this  sort of thing
 

